      "OverallSummary":"This case involves willful violations of the Care Obligation under Regulation Best Interest (Reg BI) by Nicholas Michael Caruso, a former General Securities Representative with Monmouth Capital Management LLC. Caruso recommended a series of excessive transactions in the accounts of two retail customers (Customers A and B), both in their early sixties with moderate income and net worth, that was not in either customer's best interest. The violations were determined using two key quantitative measures: (1) Cost-to-equity ratios - Customer A's exceeded 49% and Customer B's exceeded 30%, both well above the 20% threshold indicating excessive trading; and (2) Turnover rate - Customer B's was 6.56, exceeding the threshold of 6 that generally indicates excessive trading. Caruso engaged in a pattern of in-and-out, short-term trading without considering the cumulative costs. Despite Customer A having an average account balance of only $9,590, Caruso executed approximately $146,000 in securities purchases, resulting in approximately $4,500 in losses and over $7,300 in commissions and trade costs. Similarly, for Customer B with an average account balance of only $22,200, Caruso executed approximately $145,800 in securities purchases, resulting in approximately $15,800 in losses and over $6,800 in commissions and trade costs. As a result of these violations, Caruso was suspended for three months from associating with any FINRA member in all capacities. The case also notes that Monmouth Capital Management was expelled by FINRA on July 6, 2023, for churning and excessively trading customer accounts and failing to reasonably supervise its representatives' trading."
